-- title:
--   Plane graphs as faces of finite planar hypermaps
-- statement:
--   Let $G$ be a simple graph on an arbitrary finite vertex type $V$,
--   with a crossing-free plane drawing by continuous simple arcs. Then
--   $$\exists n,H:\quad H\text{ is planar and plain},\qquad
--   \exists\rho:D\to V,\quad
--   (d\sim_f d'\iff\rho(d)=\rho(d')),$$
--   $$G(v,w)\iff\exists d\in D,\quad\rho(d)=v\ \land\ \rho(e(d))=w.$$
--   Here $d\sim_f d'$ means membership in the same face orbit. Thus faces correspond
--   exactly to nonisolated vertices, and edge steps represent exactly graph adjacency.
--   Isolated vertices have no representing dart; an edgeless graph may use the empty
--   hypermap. No connectedness or nonempty-vertex assumption is imposed.
--
--   The representation's structural Lean API proves bridgelessness from looplessness
--   and transports any hypermap $k$-coloring to $G$ when $k>0$, assigning a default
--   color to isolated vertices. These facts are not assumed in the definition.
--
--   Formalization note: a paper-derived graph/dual-hypermap realization bridge,
--   not the literal topological-map discretization theorem in Rocq. It is the
--   source-backed bridge into the hypermap coloring child. The proof must construct
--   the incidence representation independently of the graph Four Color Theorem.
--
--   Notation: $D=\{0,\ldots,n-1\}$ is the dart set, $n = |D|$ its
--   size, and $p = \nu_H$ is the node permutation. Write $e,f$ for the edge and face
--   permutations, with $p(f(e(d)))=d$. Edge, node and face counts $E,N,F$ are numbers
--   of permutation orbits, including singleton orbits. The component count $C$ uses
--   the equivalence generated by all three permutations. Planarity means the exact
--   Euler equality $E+N+F=n+2C$, and connectedness means $C=1$.
--
--   A plain hypermap has a fixed-point-free involution $e$. Cubic means every node
--   orbit has size three; precubic means every node orbit has size at most three.
--   Bridgeless means $d$ and $e(d)$ never lie in the same face orbit. A $k$-coloring
--   is a map $D\to\{0,\ldots,k-1\}$ constant on face orbits and different across
--   each edge step. No requirement uses all available colors. The empty dart set
--   has zero components, is planar, and is colorable, but is not connected.
--
--   An admissible hypermap is planar, bridgeless, plain and precubic. A minimal
--   counterexample is a non-four-colorable admissible hypermap such that every
--   admissible hypermap with fewer darts is four-colorable. Minimality includes
--   all finite carriers through their enumeration by $\operatorname{Fin}(m)$;
--   cubicity, connectedness and lower face-degree bounds are not assumed.
--
--   Source: Georges Gonthier, A Computer-Checked Proof of the Four Colour Theorem (2005), https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/wp-content/uploads/2012/10/4colproof.pdf; Section 2, PDF p. 4, paragraph beginning 'Although the statement'; Section 5.6, PDF p. 48, discretization discussion (paragraph on PDF p. 48). Relevant displays are unnumbered. Pinned executable reference: https://github.com/rocq-community/fourcolor/blob/c1d6b1cd5288bea4b067aac13cdde3c18dffe018/theories/proof/discretize.v#L347; https://github.com/rocq-community/fourcolor/blob/c1d6b1cd5288bea4b067aac13cdde3c18dffe018/theories/proof/coloring.v#L135; https://github.com/rocq-community/fourcolor/blob/c1d6b1cd5288bea4b067aac13cdde3c18dffe018/theories/proof/geometry.v#L95.
-- source:
--   Georges Gonthier, A Computer-Checked Proof of the Four Colour Theorem (2005), https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/wp-content/uploads/2012/10/4colproof.pdf; Section 2, PDF p. 4, paragraph beginning 'Although the statement'; Section 5.6, PDF p. 48, discretization discussion (paragraph on PDF p. 48). Relevant displays are unnumbered. Pinned executable reference: https://github.com/rocq-community/fourcolor/blob/c1d6b1cd5288bea4b067aac13cdde3c18dffe018/theories/proof/discretize.v#L347; https://github.com/rocq-community/fourcolor/blob/c1d6b1cd5288bea4b067aac13cdde3c18dffe018/theories/proof/coloring.v#L135; https://github.com/rocq-community/fourcolor/blob/c1d6b1cd5288bea4b067aac13cdde3c18dffe018/theories/proof/geometry.v#L95.

import Definitions.Def_FourColor_GraphBridge

namespace FourColor
universe u
theorem graph_realization :
  ∀ (V : Type u) [Finite V] (G : SimpleGraph V), IsPlanar G →
    ∃ (n : ℕ) (H : Hypermap n), H.Planar ∧ H.Plain ∧ Nonempty (FaceRepresentation G H) := by sorry
end FourColor
